From: pg_prosupport
Subject: Move numeric_scaled_sum into the catalog; call its validator directly

Patch 1 (pg18-agg-simplify-hook.patch) added agg_simplify_hook, a pluggable
hook that a loaded module installs to rewrite an Aggref at plan time.
pg_prosupport used it to substitute its own, extension-defined
numeric_scaled_sum(numeric, int4) for sum(numeric).

This second patch takes the single most valuable and always-safe case out of
the extension and into core outright: numeric_scaled_sum(numeric, int4) is
now a real pg_proc/pg_aggregate catalog aggregate (oids 9523-9528, chosen
from the free range src/include/catalog/unused_oids reports), implemented in
numeric.c beside sum(numeric)'s own numeric_avg_accum/numeric_sum, and
eval_const_expressions_mutator()'s T_Aggref case (clauses.c) calls its
validator, simplify_sum_numeric_aggref(), directly and unconditionally --
one plain function call taking the Aggref and nothing else, no hook
indirection, no PlannerInfo (the substitution needs none: it is proved from
the Aggref and pg_catalog alone). There is nothing to opt into or chain: the
substitution is always correct when it fires, which is precisely why it
belongs in core rather than behind a hook a module has to install.

agg_simplify_hook itself is untouched and still runs, right after, for
whatever the direct call declined -- pg_prosupport still uses it for
avg(numeric), for sum(a*b) product-folding, and for the constant-argument
fold in constagg.c, none of which move into core here.

Where the substitution happens.  simplify_sum_numeric_aggref() recognises
aggref->aggfnoid == F_SUM_NUMERIC, declines DISTINCT, ORDER BY, FILTER,
VARIADIC and outer references exactly as any Aggref rewrite here has to, and
otherwise reads the sole argument's numeric typmod (unwrapping RelabelType
for a domain, exactly as exprTypmod() would if domains carried their typmod
through relabelling, which they do not). If 1 <= p <= 28 and 0 <= s <= p, it
substitutes numeric_scaled_sum(numeric, int4) with s as a planted constant;
otherwise it returns NULL and sum(numeric) is planned as usual. There is no
arithmetic-expression derivation here (no sum(a*b), no sum(a+b)): that stays
in the extension, which still has pps_derive_bounds() for it.

Why a catalog aggregate is worth it.  On a platform with native 128-bit
integers, numeric_scaled_sum's transition state (NumericScaledAggState) is a
single int128 mantissa and four counters -- one cache line, no pointers --
where NumericAggState carries NumericVars whose digit arrays are separately
palloc'd. hash_agg_entry_size() takes the size of a HashAgg entry from
aggtransspace in pg_aggregate.dat, so this is what moves the point at which a
GROUP BY starts to spill, not merely a constant factor on top of the same
plan. Parallel aggregation is supported through aggcombinefn/aggserialfn/
aggdeserialfn, the same as sum(numeric) itself. Where int128 is unavailable,
the transition-side functions fall back to plain NumericAggState
accumulation (ignoring the scale argument, which nothing is left to specialise
on) -- correct everywhere, specialised only where int128 exists.

One case is deliberately excluded: a bare Const argument, e.g.
sum('0'::numeric). It has its own typmod and would otherwise qualify, but
sum() over a constant does not have to be computed as a sum at all -- it can
become a plain multiplication by count(*), which is a strictly better
rewrite -- and that transformation belongs to whatever the query already
routes such Aggrefs to (agg_simplify_hook, and in pg_prosupport the
constant-fold in constagg.c), not to this substitution getting there first
and taking the choice away.

Verification.  All 231 tests in `make check` under src/test/regress pass
unchanged; that exercises sum(numeric) extensively (aggregates, numeric,
partition_aggregate, join, subselect, window and more), and the substitution
is silent in EXPLAIN except for the aggregate's name. Beyond that: NaN and
+/-Infinity inputs; DISTINCT and FILTER correctly left unsubstituted; the
p = 28 boundary with a 28-digit value; parallel aggregation, whose combined
result was checked byte-for-byte against the same query planned serially
(max_parallel_workers_per_gather = 0); a bare numeric constant confirmed to
still reach agg_simplify_hook rather than being substituted here; and a wide
sum (dscale 4, 300000 rows) landing well past int64, to exercise the
three-chunk int64_div_fast_to_numeric() reconstruction in
numeric_scaled_sum_final() -- an earlier version of this patch mishandled
that path, tacking the scale onto an integer-valued NumericVar as a dscale
rather than actually dividing by it, and building pg_prosupport against the
result (see its own updated regression tests) is what caught it.

Tested against PostgreSQL 18.6, on top of
pg18-agg-simplify-hook.patch (patch 1). Apply from the top of a PostgreSQL
source tree, after patch 1, with:

    patch -p1 < contrib/pg_prosupport/patches/pg18-agg-simplify-hook.patch
    patch -p1 < contrib/pg_prosupport/patches/pg18-numeric-scaled-sum-catalog.patch

or, inside a git checkout, with git apply in the same order. A build from
this tree requires a fresh initdb: new pg_proc/pg_aggregate rows change the
system catalogs, so an existing data directory from before the patch cannot
be reused.

Also adds src/backend/utils/adt/README.numeric_scaled_sum, a plain-text
subsystem README in the usual PostgreSQL style: what NumericScaledAggState
buys over NumericAggState and why, where the mantissa extraction avoids
building an intermediate Numeric, why the final reconstruction is allowed to
be comparatively expensive (it runs once per group, not once per row), and
how this relates to the further specialisations (product folding, avg,
arithmetic-expression arguments) that pg_prosupport itself still provides
through agg_simplify_hook.
---
